    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>There are 28 known types of collagen in the human body, and only three of them
        matter when you are choosing a supplement.</strong> Type 1 builds skin, bone and tendon and
        accounts for roughly 90% of all the collagen you carry. Type 2 is cartilage. Type 3 sits
        alongside type 1 in skin and blood vessels. Before you pay extra for a number on a label:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Type 1 is the workhorse.</strong> Around 90% of body collagen, and the type in almost every marine collagen powder or shot sold for skin, bone and tendon.</li>
        <li><strong>Type 2 exists at two completely different doses.</strong> Undenatured type 2 (UC-II) is studied at 40mg daily and works through the immune system. Hydrolysed collagen is studied at 5,000mg to 10,000mg and works through a different route entirely.</li>
        <li><strong>Hydrolysis erases the type.</strong> Once collagen is broken into peptides, the triple helix is gone. A hydrolysed "type 2" product behaves like any other collagen peptide.</li>
        <li><strong>Honest limitation:</strong> no trial has shown that swallowing a particular collagen type sends those peptides preferentially to the matching tissue. The body-part matching story is marketing, not physiology.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

