      <h2 id="how-many">How many types of collagen are there?</h2>

      <p>
        Twenty eight. That is the number of genetically distinct collagen types identified in
        vertebrates so far, and if you have never heard of type 17 or type 24, that is because they
        exist in quantities and locations that have nothing to do with anything you can buy.
      </p>

      <p>
        Collagen is the structural protein that holds the body together. It is a rope: three long
        amino acid chains wound into a triple helix, then bundled with other helices into fibrils,
        then into fibres strong enough to anchor a tendon to bone. Roughly every third amino acid in
        that chain is glycine, the smallest one, because nothing bigger fits at the centre of the
        twist. Proline and hydroxyproline sit at the other positions and lock the helix in place.
        This is why collagen looks so odd as a protein and why it behaves so differently from whey
        or dal protein in the gut.
      </p>

      <p>
        The 28 types differ in which genes code those chains, how the fibrils assemble, and where in
        the body they end up. Type 4 forms sheets rather than ropes and makes up the basement
        membrane under your skin. Type 5 helps organise type 1 fibrils. Type 10 appears in growing
        bone at the growth plate. Interesting biology, no commercial relevance.
      </p>

      <p>
        For anyone standing in a pharmacy comparing tubs, the useful number is three. Types 1, 2 and
        3 together account for the overwhelming majority of collagen by mass in an adult body, and
        they are the only ones any supplement company has ever bothered to put in a capsule.
      </p>

