      <h2 id="type-1">Type 1 collagen: skin, bone and tendon</h2>

      <p>
        Type 1 collagen is the most abundant protein in the human body, full stop. It makes up
        around 90% of your total collagen and is the main structural fibre in skin, bone, tendon,
        ligament, the sclera of your eye and the wall of your gut.
      </p>

      <p>
        In orthopaedic terms, type 1 is what I spend most of my working life dealing with. Bone is
        roughly one third organic matrix by weight, and about 90% of that matrix is type 1 collagen.
        The mineral phase gives bone its hardness. The collagen gives it the toughness not to
        shatter when you land awkwardly. Osteoporotic bone is not simply low on calcium; the
        collagen scaffold is degraded too, which is part of why calcium supplements alone do so
        little for fracture risk.
      </p>

      <p>
        Tendons and ligaments are almost pure type 1, arranged in near parallel bundles that make
        them extraordinarily strong along one axis and vulnerable to twisting. Skin dermis is
        predominantly type 1 with type 3 interwoven, which is the pairing that gives young skin both
        tensile strength and pliability.
      </p>

      <p>
        Marine collagen, the kind extracted from fish skin and scales, is type 1. So is bovine hide
        collagen, mostly, with some type 3 alongside. If you have been reading about
        <a href="/pages/marine-collagen">marine collagen</a> or comparing it with the bovine
        version, you are effectively comparing two type 1 sources with different peptide profiles
        and very different dietary acceptability in an Indian household. That comparison is covered
        properly in <a href="/pages/marine-vs-bovine-collagen">marine vs bovine collagen</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="type-2">Collagen type 2: cartilage and the UC-II story</h2>

      <p>
        Type 2 collagen is the collagen of cartilage. It is the fibre network inside the glassy
        surface capping the ends of your bones, and it is the only one of the three main types with
        a genuinely distinct supplement story, because type 2 is sold at two doses that differ by a
        factor of more than two hundred.
      </p>

      <p>
        Articular cartilage is roughly 70% water, held in a mesh of type 2 collagen fibrils and
        proteoglycans. The collagen provides tensile restraint; the proteoglycans pull in water and
        provide the cushioning. That mesh has no blood supply of its own, which is why cartilage
        heals so poorly and why joint problems behave nothing like a muscle strain.
      </p>

      <h3>Undenatured type 2 collagen at 40mg</h3>

      <p>
        Undenatured type 2 collagen, usually sold as UC-II, is type 2 that has been extracted from
        chicken sternum cartilage with its triple helix left intact. It is not broken down, not
        heated, not hydrolysed. The proposed mechanism has nothing to do with supplying building
        blocks. It is oral tolerance: small amounts of intact type 2 collagen presented to immune
        tissue in the small intestine appear to damp down the immune response that attacks the
        body's own joint cartilage.
      </p>

      <p>
        Lugo and colleagues reported in <em>Nutrition Journal</em> in 2016 that 40mg of UC-II daily
        improved knee pain and function scores in adults with knee osteoarthritis over 180 days.
        Forty milligrams. That is a rounding error next to the gram doses used in peptide trials,
        and it works precisely because the molecule stays whole.
      </p>

      <p>
        The catch is fragility. Heat destroys the effect, and so does hydrolysis. Any processing
        that unwinds the helix converts UC-II into ordinary denatured collagen protein, at a dose
        far too small to matter as a peptide.
      </p>

      <h3>Hydrolysed collagen at 5g to 10g</h3>

      <p>
        The second route is the opposite approach. Collagen is deliberately broken down into short
        peptides, taken in gram quantities, absorbed into the blood, and appears to signal
        chondrocytes and fibroblasts to increase matrix production. Trials here used 5,000mg to
        10,000mg daily for three to six months. I have gone through that evidence in detail in
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-joint-pain">collagen for joint pain</a>, including where it
        holds up and where it does not.
      </p>

      <p>
        These two mechanisms are not competing versions of the same thing. They are different
        interventions that happen to share a word on the label. Neither is universally better, and a
        product promising both in one bottle is describing something chemically awkward, since the
        processing that creates peptides is exactly the processing that destroys UC-II.
      </p>

