      <h2 id="type-3">Type 3 collagen: the one that travels with type 1</h2>

      <p>
        Type 3 collagen is a thinner, more flexible fibre found in skin, blood vessel walls, the
        gut and internal organs, and it almost always occurs alongside type 1 rather than on its
        own. It is sometimes called reticular collagen because of the fine network it forms.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two things about type 3 are worth knowing. First, it is the collagen the body lays down
        first when repairing a wound; the early scar is type 3 rich, and over the following months
        it is gradually remodelled into stronger type 1. Second, the ratio of type 3 to type 1 in
        skin falls with age, which is one of the structural changes behind thinner, less elastic
        skin in your forties compared with your twenties.
      </p>

      <p>
        You will see "type 1 and 3" on a great many skin collagen labels. That pairing is honest
        enough as a description of what is in bovine hide, and it reflects real dermal biology. It
        does not mean the product delivers type 3 collagen to your dermis. It means the raw material
        contained both before it was hydrolysed. For what oral collagen genuinely does to skin, see
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-skin">collagen for skin</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="which-type">Which type of collagen should you take?</h2>

      <p>
        For skin, hair and nails, choose a type 1 source at 2.5g to 10g daily. For joints you have a
        real choice between two mechanisms: hydrolysed peptides at 5g to 10g, or undenatured type 2
        at 40mg. For bone, type 1 peptides at 5g, because bone matrix is type 1.
      </p>

