        That last row is not a throwaway. India is a substantially vegetarian market and there is no
        such thing as vegan collagen, only vegan "collagen builders" that supply vitamin C, glycine,
        proline and amino acids and hope your fibroblasts do the rest. Those products can be
        reasonable. They are not collagen, and a label that implies otherwise is misleading you.
      </p>

      <h2 id="hydrolysed">Does hydrolyzed collagen still have a type?</h2>

      <p>
        Technically yes, practically no. Hydrolysis chops the triple helix into short peptide
        fragments of roughly 2,000 to 5,000 daltons, and once that has happened the structural
        identity that made it type 1 or type 2 no longer exists in any functional sense.
      </p>

      <p>
        Think about what hydrolysis actually is. Enzymes cut the long chains at specific points. The
        helix unwinds. What reaches your bloodstream is a mixture of di- and tripeptides, most
        famously prolyl-hydroxyproline and hydroxyprolyl-glycine, plus free amino acids. Those
        fragments are essentially the same regardless of whether the starting material was fish
        skin, cow hide or chicken cartilage, because all collagen types are built from the same
        limited amino acid vocabulary. The differences between types live in sequence and assembly,
        and both are destroyed by the enzyme.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is the single most useful thing to understand about the whole category, so let me put
        it bluntly. <strong>A product selling you gram quantities of "type 2 collagen" is selling
        you hydrolysed peptides with a type 2 origin story.</strong> Those peptides may well work,
        the joint trials suggest they do, but they work as peptides, not as type 2. The number is
        provenance, not mechanism. More on how the peptides themselves behave in
        <a href="/pages/collagen-peptides">collagen peptides</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The exception, and it is the only real one, is UC-II. Its whole point is that it is not
        hydrolysed. If a label says undenatured, and the dose is around 40mg, the type designation
        is genuinely load bearing.
      </p>

