      <h2 id="myth">Do you need to match the type number to the body part?</h2>

      <p>
        No. There is no evidence that swallowed collagen of a given type is preferentially delivered
        to the tissue that contains that type. Your digestive tract does not read labels, and the
        peptides that cross into your blood do not carry a routing instruction.
      </p>

      <p>
        The matching myth is intuitive, which is why it sells. Cartilage is type 2, therefore knees
        need type 2. Skin is type 1, therefore faces need type 1. The problem is that it describes
        the destination tissue rather than the delivered molecule. Once peptides are absorbed they
        circulate, and the trials that reported benefit did not do so because the right type reached
        the right place. They reported benefit because peptide fragments appear to act as signals,
        prompting fibroblasts and chondrocytes to increase their own matrix production, whichever
        cells happen to be in a state to respond.
      </p>

      <p>
        The best evidence against the matching story is the joint trials themselves. Clark and
        colleagues gave 147 athletes 10g of collagen hydrolysate daily for 24 weeks and recorded
        significant reductions in joint pain when walking, standing and carrying objects. That
        hydrolysate was not undenatured type 2. It was ordinary collagen peptides, and the joints
        responded anyway. In the other direction, König and colleagues found in 2018 that 5g of
        collagen peptides daily for 12 months raised bone mineral density in postmenopausal women,
        again without any type matching.
      </p>

      <p>
        So the practical rule is simpler than the marketing. Judge a collagen product on the dose in
        milligrams, the source, whether it is hydrolysed or undenatured, and what co-factors sit
        alongside it. The type number is close to the least informative thing on the label unless
        that label says undenatured.
      </p>

