      <h2 id="practical">What I actually tell patients</h2>

      <p>
        Pick the mechanism first, then the dose, then worry about the type. In OPD that conversation
        takes about ninety seconds and it saves people from buying a 60mg "type 2" capsule that is
        neither undenatured nor a meaningful peptide dose.
      </p>

      <p>
        If someone has activity related knee or shoulder discomfort and no diagnosis, I point them
        at hydrolysed peptides at 5g to 10g daily for at least twelve weeks. If someone has
        radiologically confirmed osteoarthritis and wants to try the immune route, UC-II at 40mg is
        a legitimate and separately evidenced option, and it is not something we make. If someone is
        primarily worried about bone, type 1 peptides plus vitamin D and calcium, because vitamin D
        deficiency runs at 70% to 90% in urban Indian adults and no amount of collagen compensates
        for that.
      </p>

      <p>
        Where our own formula sits should be equally plain. <a href="/products/skelecoll">Skelecoll</a>
        uses 10,000mg of hydrolysed Japanese marine collagen, which is type 1 in origin and peptide
        in behaviour, matched to the 10g dose in the Clark trial. It contains no UC-II. If the
        undenatured type 2 mechanism is what you specifically want, buy a UC-II product at 40mg and
        judge it on its own terms over three to six months. I would rather say that than sell you
        something on a technicality.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two more honest limits. The glucosamine in Skelecoll is 300mg, well below the 1,500mg used
        in glucosamine monotherapy trials, so it is a co-factor in a stack and not a standalone
        glucosamine therapy. And collagen of any type will not repair a torn meniscus, will not
        treat inflammatory arthritis, and will not rebuild lost cartilage. A joint that is hot, red,
        swollen, locking or waking you at night needs a doctor rather than a supplement.
      </p>

