      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>What are the 3 main types of collagen?</summary>
          <p>
            Type 1, type 2 and type 3. Type 1 is about 90% of your body's collagen and builds skin,
            bone, tendon and ligament. Type 2 is the collagen of cartilage. Type 3 is a finer fibre
            in skin, blood vessels and organs that almost always occurs alongside type 1.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which type of collagen is best for skin?</summary>
          <p>
            Type 1, usually from marine or bovine sources, at 2.5g to 10g of hydrolysed peptides
            daily for 8 to 12 weeks. Skin dermis is mostly type 1 with type 3 woven through it, and
            the skin trials used type 1 rich hydrolysates. Note that the peptides are absorbed as
            fragments, so the type is a description of the raw material rather than a delivery
            mechanism.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do I need collagen type 2 for my knees?</summary>
          <p>
            Not necessarily. Undenatured type 2 at 40mg daily has its own evidence in knee
            osteoarthritis through an immune tolerance pathway, but hydrolysed collagen peptides at
            5g to 10g daily also reduced joint pain in trials without being type 2. Choose the
            mechanism and dose you want rather than the number on the front of the pack.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What is undenatured type 2 collagen (UC-II)?</summary>
          <p>
            UC-II is type 2 collagen extracted from chicken sternum cartilage with its triple helix
            intact. It is taken at around 40mg daily and appears to work by training the immune
            system to stop attacking joint cartilage. Heating or hydrolysing it destroys the effect,
            so it cannot be combined with a gram level peptide dose in the same process.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does hydrolyzed collagen still count as type 1 or type 2?</summary>
          <p>
            Only as a description of where it came from. Hydrolysis breaks the triple helix into
            peptides of roughly 2,000 to 5,000 daltons, and at that point the type specific
            structure no longer exists. Hydrolysed type 2 behaves like any other collagen peptide in
            the body.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I take type 1 and type 2 collagen together?</summary>
          <p>
            Yes, there is no known interaction and they act through different routes. In practice
            that means a gram level type 1 peptide dose plus a separate 40mg undenatured type 2
            capsule, since the two cannot be produced in the same process. Give any combination at
            least 12 weeks before deciding whether it is doing anything.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

